# Grow Node MVP Rules

## Source of truth

- Read `docs/project-scope.md` before proposing or making changes.
- Treat the MVP scope, release checklist, definition of done, and stop rule as authoritative.
- Keep `README.md` accurate when setup steps or visible demo behaviour change.
- Do not expand the project merely because an additional feature is technically possible.

## Project goal

Grow Node is a portfolio-quality, local-first IoT monitoring demo.

The public MVP must run locally with `docker compose up --build`, require no physical hardware or cloud account, and show fresh simulated telemetry in the dashboard.

The primary demo uses one simulated zone and three core readings: temperature, humidity, and light.

Do not add AWS, authentication, cloud deployment, multi-tenant features, production IoT provisioning, advanced analytics, alerts, AI recommendations, or extra services unless the user explicitly changes the MVP scope.

## Current release priorities

Work only toward the unchecked items in the release checklist unless fixing a demonstrated defect.

- Keep the default demo healthy and understandable with one active simulated zone.
- Clarify the dashboard hierarchy without removing existing profile metrics or evaluation data.
- Add complete plant-profile management with safe assignment and deletion behaviour.
- Remove temporary debug UI and unused components.
- Preserve the one-command Docker startup, automatic migrations, and idempotent seed data.
- Keep optional ESP32 firmware separate from the required local demo.
- Prefer clearer grouping and labels over removing domain data.

## Architecture

Local data flow:

simulator or optional ESP32 firmware -> Mosquitto MQTT -> control service -> PostgreSQL -> API service -> Next.js dashboard

Main folders:

- `apps/dashboard` — Next.js dashboard
- `services/api` — dashboard-facing HTTP API
- `services/control` — MQTT subscriber, decision engine, database writes
- `services/simulator` — fake ESP32 telemetry publisher
- `packages/contracts` — Zod schemas, MQTT topics, shared API/MQTT types
- `packages/db` — Prisma schema and database client
- `firmware/grow-node-zone` — ESP32 firmware, not part of pnpm workspace
- `docs` — architecture and development documentation

## Code rules

- Use TypeScript for all Node.js and Next.js code.
- Do not use `any`.
- Use Zod for external boundaries.
- Validate MQTT payloads before using them.
- Validate API params and request bodies before using them.
- Keep shared MQTT/API contracts in `packages/contracts`.
- Keep Prisma schema and database client in `packages/db`.
- Do not duplicate shared payload types across services.
- Prefer simple readable types over complex generic types.

## Dashboard rules

- Use Next.js App Router.
- Prefer server components by default.
- Use client components only for interactivity.
- Dashboard communicates with `services/api` over HTTP.
- Dashboard must not import Prisma.
- Dashboard must not connect directly to MQTT.
- Dashboard must not contain control-service decision logic.
- The overview must emphasise temperature, humidity, light, controller freshness, and desired fan/light state.
- The detail view may retain VPD, estimated PPFD, condensation margin, settling timers, and complete metric evaluation.
- Plant-profile forms must preserve the complete `lightOn` and `lightOff` configuration.
- The default simulator-only demo must not appear degraded because optional hardware is offline.
- Prefer a calm, readable desktop layout over dense or decorative visualisation.

## Plant-profile rules

- One plant profile may be assigned to multiple zones.
- Profile create and update requests must use shared Zod contracts.
- Deleting a profile that is used by any zone must return `409 Conflict` before attempting deletion.
- The conflict response must include the IDs and names of every affected zone so the dashboard can link to them.
- Keep the database relation restrictive as a second layer of protection.
- Do not add authentication or user roles for the local MVP unless explicitly requested.

## API service rules

- API owns dashboard-facing HTTP access.
- API validates requests with Zod.
- API reads dashboard data from PostgreSQL.
- API can create manual command records.
- API can publish manual fan/light commands to MQTT.
- API does not consume telemetry from MQTT.

## Control service rules

- Control service owns telemetry ingestion.
- Control service subscribes to telemetry, heartbeat, and command acknowledgement topics.
- Control service validates incoming MQTT payloads.
- Control service stores telemetry readings.
- Control service updates latest zone state.
- Control service evaluates plant profile thresholds.
- Control service can publish automatic commands.
- Control service processes command acknowledgements.

## Simulator rules

- Simulator behaves like fake ESP32 hardware.
- Use plain Node.js TypeScript.
- Do not add Fastify to the simulator unless explicitly requested.
- Simulator publishes telemetry and heartbeat messages over MQTT.
- Simulator listens for commands and publishes command acknowledgements.
- The default demo simulates one zone.
- Physical hardware must never be required to review the public MVP.

## Infrastructure rules

- Docker Compose must remain the supported reviewer workflow.
- A fresh database must receive migrations and idempotent seed data automatically.
- `grow-node-db-setup` is a one-shot service and should exit successfully.
- Do not reintroduce bind mounts that hide image-installed pnpm workspace dependencies in the default Compose file.
- Preserve PostgreSQL data when running `docker compose down`; use volume deletion only when a deliberate reset is requested.

## Testing rules

Testing should stay small and practical.

Prioritize:

- contract schema validation tests
- control service decision logic tests
- API command validation tests

Before public release, run:

- `pnpm format:check`
- `pnpm lint`
- `pnpm typecheck`
- `pnpm test`

Do not add Playwright, Testcontainers, real MQTT integration tests, or complex E2E tests in the first version.

## Workflow

- Finish each independently verifiable implementation step with a focused Git commit.
- Before committing, run the relevant checks and review `git diff` and `git status` for unrelated or generated files.
- Keep documentation, implementation, and refactoring in separate commits when they represent distinct steps.
- Write commit subjects that describe the completed outcome; do not use `wip`, `fix`, `update`, `changes`, or similarly vague messages.
- Do not commit secrets, local environment files, generated caches, or unrelated user changes.
- Keep commits small and descriptive.
- Before large changes, propose a short plan.
- Update docs when architecture, setup commands, API routes, or MQTT contracts change.
- Do not expand scope without updating `docs/project-scope.md`.
- When every definition-of-done item is satisfied, stop adding features and prepare the repository for publication.
